==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|absolute|ness}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈab.sə.luːt.nəs/|/ab.səˈluːt.nəs/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-absoluteness.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.səˌlut.nəs/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-absoluteness.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The fact of being [[finished]] or [[perfected]]; [[completeness]]. {{defdate|Attested from the mid 16<sup>th</sup> century until the 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}
# The characteristic of being [[absolute]] in [[nature]] or [[scope]].
#: {{co|en|the '''absoluteness''' of his sovereignty, the '''absoluteness''' of his convictions}}
# Absolute [[authority]], unlimited power; [[absolutism]], [[despotism]]. {{defdate|First attested in the 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=8|book=II|page=228|passage=I know no man that could produce more parts, both naturall and artificiall, fit to preserve his masterie, and to maintaine his '''absolutenesse''', than he doth; yet is hee cleane falne from them like a childe.}}
# The fact of being without qualifications or conditions; [[certainty]], unconditionality. {{defdate|First attested in the 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}
# Independent [[autonomy]]. {{defdate|First attested in the 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|unconditionality}}
* {{l|en|unlimitedness}}
* {{l|en|certainty}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|conditionality}}
* {{l|en|limitedness}}
* {{l|en|relativity}}

